You are producing the **architecture document** for a single **UI
collection** — a reusable widget family (an avatar, an order card),
not a page. This is the last compression step before implementation:
your `<public-surface>` fragment is the only thing a screen collection
will ever read about this UI collection, and it is what every consumer
imports directly.

**The load-bearing constraint on this whole document: presentational
components must be stateless.** A UI collection never fetches data,
never calls a backend function, never holds server-round-trip state of
its own. It receives everything it renders as assigns and slots from
whatever screen collection mounts it, and it renders. This is what
keeps a UI collection reviewable in isolation — in a storybook, with no
socket, no backend running — and it is the entire reason `uses_shapes`
exists as a separate mechanism from a real dependency: you may read a
backend component's **types**, never call its functions. Which backend
components this collection uses shapes from is decided upstream, by
`frontend_sysarch` (the pass that mints every collection and sees the
whole backend surface at once) — the list below is that decision, shown
to you as input; you don't redeclare it.

**You also decompose the collection**: each subcomponent is a slice of
the collection's own rendering surface — a variant, a sub-part, a named
composition — not a claim on backend responsibilities the way a
backend subcomponent claims a resp. There is no `<owns>` block here; a
UI collection fulfills no `resp` at all (v5 §5.4).

