Your job is to produce a single `<ui_collarch>` block containing nine
sections in a fixed order: a role-level technical specification, the
collection's public surface, its private surface, its failure surface,
what it mints as policy considerations, its design-system primitives,
its subcomponent decomposition, the dependency edges between
subcomponents, and attached references.

# Output format

Emit exactly one `<ui_collarch>` block with these children in this
order: `<technical-specification>` → `<public-surface>` →
`<private-surface>` → `<failure-surface>` → `<policies>` →
`<primitives>` → `<subcomponents>` →
`<sub-dependencies>` → `<references>` (optional). Example (abbreviated):

    <ui_collarch>
      <technical-specification>
    A function-component family with no internal state and no
    lifecycle hooks. Every prop is either primitive data or a slot;
    nothing here issues a network call or subscribes to anything.
      </technical-specification>
      <public-surface>
    ```
    Avatar(assigns) — assigns: %{user: %{name, image_url, presence},
    size: :sm | :md | :lg}. Renders an image with a presence-colored
    ring; falls back to initials when image_url is nil.

    slot :badge — optional overlay content (a role indicator, an
    unread-count pill).
    ```
      </public-surface>
      <private-surface>
    ```
    initials(name) -> String.t() — internal helper, not exported.
    ```
      </private-surface>
      <failure-surface>
    A missing or unreachable image_url silently falls back to
    initials — this is intended, not a residual risk to fix. A
    presence value outside the known enum renders with no ring color
    rather than raising, since a stale client is expected to send one
    occasionally.
      </failure-surface>
      <policies>
    No collection-local policy considerations.
      </policies>
      <primitives>
        <design-system to="design_system01"/>
      </primitives>
      <subcomponents>
        <subcomponent alias="avatar_image">
          <name>AvatarImage</name>
          <purpose>Renders the image-or-initials fallback and the
          presence ring.</purpose>
          <owned-invariants>
            <invariant>a missing image always falls back to initials,
            never a broken-image icon</invariant>
            <invariant>the presence ring color is a pure function of
            the presence value</invariant>
          </owned-invariants>
          <primary-operations>
            <operation>render the image or initials fallback</operation>
            <operation>render the presence ring</operation>
          </primary-operations>
          <responsibilities>The leaf rendering logic for one avatar;
          the parent component composes this with the optional badge
          slot.</responsibilities>
          <implementation grade="real"/>
        </subcomponent>
      </subcomponents>
      <sub-dependencies>
      </sub-dependencies>
    </ui_collarch>

# Rules

## Structure

* Emit **exactly one** `<ui_collarch>` root block. Nothing before,
  nothing after.
* The children **must appear in this order**: `<technical-specification>`
  → `<public-surface>` → `<private-surface>` → `<failure-surface>` →
  `<policies>` → `<primitives>` → `<subcomponents>`
  → `<sub-dependencies>` → `<references>` (omit entirely when nothing
  is attached). Out-of-order sections are a structural error.

## Fragment sections

* `<technical-specification>`, `<public-surface>`, `<private-surface>`,
  `<failure-surface>` and `<policies>` are all non-empty prose — the
  same fragment discipline the backend `comparch` tier uses
  (`<public-surface>` in fenced code blocks for signatures, prose
  elsewhere). `<policies>` states cross-cutting rendering conventions
  this collection is a candidate for (a required accessibility pattern,
  a required loading-state convention) or states plainly that none
  apply.
* **No backend call ever belongs in `<public-surface>` or
  `<private-surface>`.** If you find yourself describing a fetch, a
  boundary-export call, or anything that waits on a network round
  trip, that behavior belongs in a screen collection, not here — name
  the data shape you need as an assign instead.

## Primitives

* `<primitives>` carries at most one `<design-system to="..."/>` —
  include it only when the project supplied a design system (shown
  above) and this collection actually builds on its primitives rather
  than defining its own from scratch. Omit `<primitives></primitives>`
  content (leave it empty) when no design system was supplied or this
  collection doesn't use one.
* **No dependency edges are declared here.** Which sibling UI
  collections this one composes (`<ui-dependencies>`) and which backend
  components it reads shapes from (`uses_shapes`) are both
  `frontend_sysarch`'s own decisions, made when every collection and
  the whole backend surface are in view at once — you read both back as
  input (the sibling list and the backend-shapes list above) to inform
  `<technical-specification>`/`<public-surface>`, you don't redeclare
  either structurally. **Never a call to a backend component in
  `<technical-specification>` or `<public-surface>`** — a backend shape
  you read is types/handle shapes only, never invoked (v5 §5.4).

## Subcomponents

* Each `<subcomponent>` carries an `alias`, `<name>`, `<purpose>`,
  `<owned-invariants>` (2-4), `<primary-operations>` (3-6),
  `<responsibilities>` (free prose — what this piece renders and how
  it composes with its siblings), and `<implementation grade=
  "real"|"stubbed"/>` (the same stub-grade doctrine every architecture
  tier in this chain uses — a stub still needs `<swap>` and
  `<exit-plan>`).
* No `<owns>` block: a UI collection's subcomponents are a rendering
  decomposition (a variant, a slot's own sub-tree, a shared visual
  primitive local to this collection), not a claim on backend resps.
* Decompose along **rendering seams** — a distinct visual state, a
  reusable sub-part several variants share, a slot's own composition —
  the UI-collection analogue of the data/operation seams a backend
  subcomponent splits along.

## Sub-dependencies

* `<sub-dependencies>` lists edges between this collection's own
  subcomponents, `<dep from="alias" to="alias"/>` using local aliases.
  Must be acyclic; a cycle is a structural error fed back on retry.

## References

* `<references>` is optional — omit entirely when nothing is attached.
  When present, lists `<reference target="ref_..."/>` for every
  attached ref shown above.

## Meta-rules

* Do not include commentary about what you are doing or how you
  arrived at the decomposition. Output only the `<ui_collarch>` block.
* Unescaped `&` and `<` inside fragment-section text are tolerated by
  the parser.
